      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The 4-ablation budget arithmetic is now correct (worst-case 171 H100-hours, under 200), and the mutation validation protocol is well-designed. However, the core 22h/run training estimate remains indirectly grounded: the Qwen2.5-Coder technical report confirms A100-80GB hardware class but gives no QLoRA training times, and SWE-smith's 'single-digit-day' cite covers full-parameter fine-tuning at a different scale. The ≥95% inclusion rate target for all five operators is stated as a target without evidence that statement reordering and loop normalization — where conservative dependency analysis may filter aggressively — will actually reach this threshold, which affects training data volume and InfoNCE batch quality."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The proposal is well-organized, differentiation from all major competitors is explicit and accurate, the ablation design is coherent, and the RGFL comparison fallback is clearly stated. Minor issue: the ≥95% inclusion rate is stated as a target as if it were verified, when it is an aspirational bound that likely varies substantially across operators."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Surface-form robustness in fault localization is a documented, specific problem with practical downstream consequences (confirmed by arXiv 2604.05481's causal chain from localization accuracy to repair success), and the proposal's downstream repair validation closes the 'so what' loop. The EMNLP 2026 robustness/trustworthiness theme is a direct fit. Impact score is limited to 4 because the actual magnitude of robustness gain is unknown, and the fault localization framing may still read as SE-primary to NLP reviewers despite the correct surface-form-brittleness framing."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "MINOR — TRAINING TIME ESTIMATE STILL INSUFFICIENTLY GROUNDED: The ~22h/run estimate for Qwen2.5-Coder-32B QLoRA on 2×A100-80GB cites (a) the Qwen2.5-Coder technical report as confirming A100-80GB hardware class (not training time) and (b) SWE-smith as showing full-parameter fine-tuning is feasible 'within single-digit-day timescales' — which is a substantially weaker grounding than a specific QLoRA timing benchmark. The Alibaba Cloud Qwen2.5-Coder fine-tuning tutorial (alibabacloud.com/help/en/pai/use-cases/training-evaluation-compression-and-deployment-of-qwen2-5-coder-model) and the Hugging Face Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct discussion thread contain specific hardware-time data that directly supports or refutes the 22h estimate. The ±50% variance buffer is the correct risk mitigation, but the underlying estimate should be anchored to a concrete source to survive reviewer feasibility scrutiny.",
    "MINOR — ≥95% INCLUSION RATE TARGET IS UNSTRATIFIED: The proposal states a single ≥95% inclusion rate target for all five operators, but operator risk varies dramatically: variable renaming and alias introduction are safe by construction and will approach 100%, dead-code insertion is similarly safe, while statement reordering and loop normalization depend on conservative dependency analysis that may filter a large fraction of candidate statement blocks. Stating a uniform 95% target obscures that the effective training data from reordering/loop normalization operators may be substantially smaller. The proposal should acknowledge that per-operator inclusion rates will differ and that a smaller-than-expected contribution from complex operators does not invalidate the InfoNCE objective — it just reduces operator diversity for those categories. This is a framing fix, not a scientific problem."
  ],
  "feedback": "Round 3's three issues (mutation validity, budget arithmetic, ablation count) are all correctly resolved. The proposal is scientifically sound and the remaining issues are minor framing and grounding problems. Two targeted fixes bring this to pass:\n\n1. **Anchor the 22h/run estimate to a direct source**: Fetch the Alibaba Cloud Qwen2.5-Coder fine-tuning tutorial or cite the Hugging Face Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct hardware discussion thread for a specific QLoRA training time on 2×A100-80GB. If no direct source is available, replace the citation with: 'The 22h/run estimate is derived from first-principles: [sequence length] × [dataset size] × [QLoRA optimizer steps] at measured [tokens/sec] throughput on A100-80GB (citing any community QLoRA benchmark for a comparable 30B-class model).' The ±50% variance already provides adequate safety margin — the fix is about the base estimate's citation.\n\n2. **Stratify the inclusion rate claim by operator risk**: Change '≥95% inclusion rate per operator' to 'Variable renaming and alias introduction satisfy the equivalence check by construction (targeting ~100% inclusion); dead-code insertion is safe by construction; statement reordering and loop normalization are restricted to dependency-free blocks and are expected to achieve lower inclusion rates depending on program structure — but these are supplementary operators and any reduction in their coverage does not affect the soundness of the InfoNCE objective or the training signal from the remaining three operators.' This makes the claim defensible rather than aspirational.",
  "reason": "All critical issues resolved; two minor grounding/framing issues prevent average from reaching the 4.0 pass threshold (current avg 3.75) — one more focused revision should reach pass."
}
